{
  "abstract": "We test whether expert iteration (EI) at scale gives a held-out greedy gain on Qwen3-0.6B-Base. From the supplied RLVR checkpoint we sample k = 8 at T 0.8 on 6,400 training questions, keep verified EOS-terminated solutions and fine-tune on them, and compare with the start and with stabilized large-batch RLVR (overlong masking) trained on the same 6,400 questions. Design, selection and gates were fixed before results: a 350-question validation split for selection, a disjoint 1,000-question confirmation panel, and MATH-500 held out. On the confirmation panel, three distillation seeds score 481, 498 and 503 against the start's 480: +1.40 points [−0.43, 3.23] (item bootstrap), seed-axis t interval [−1.46, 4.26]. The prespecified positive fails and MATH-500 was not opened. Greedy validation accuracy does not increase over 640, 1,600 and 6,400 distillation questions. The selected RLVR snapshot is no better (486/1000, +0.6 [−2.0, 3.2]). Both methods sharpen the sampled policy (mean@8 +2.5 points for each at seed 0; +3.0 over three EI seeds, t interval [1.7, 4.4]; start-vs-start floor +0.3); greedy accuracy barely follows, and neither raises the start's low-temperature vote (the comparator lowers it). Two prespecified RLVR variants aimed at the greedy decision, T 0.4 rollouts and the policy's greedy decode added to each group, also fail their gates; the same recipe run from Base, with 128 times the supplied run's rollouts, lands in the start's range. A fitting probe shows EI can fit its own frontier questions without held-out transfer. A plurality vote over 13 same-start runs reaches 190.8/350 against a member mean of 154.3, but eight T 0.4 samples of the start checkpoint itself already vote to 186.7, so the run vote is mostly self-consistency; weight soups, token-level mixtures and the start's own Yes/No self-judgement do not capture it. An erratum corrects an extraction bug that had understated sampled votes.",
